regexec.c: the map search steps through UTF-8 a byte at a time

forward_search finds where a match can start with map_search when the
regex can begin with one of several bytes. map_search stepped from
character to character, looking up each character's length. In valid
UTF-8, the characters' first bytes are exactly the bytes that are not
continuation bytes (0x80-0xBF), so for UTF-8 it now steps one byte at a
time with no length lookup and skips hits on continuation bytes. It
tests the same positions in the same order and returns the same
position. Other encodings take the old loop.

See rust/vendor/README.md.

diff --git a/oniguruma/src/regexec.c b/oniguruma/src/regexec.c
index 2e78b8a..43a3f0c 100644
--- a/oniguruma/src/regexec.c
+++ b/oniguruma/src/regexec.c
@@ -5385,6 +5385,18 @@ map_search(OnigEncoding enc, UChar map[],
 {
   const UChar *s = text;
 
+  if (enc == ONIG_ENCODING_UTF8) {
+    /* In valid UTF-8 from a character head, the character heads are exactly
+       the bytes that are not continuation bytes (0x80-0xBF): step one byte
+       at a time, with no length lookup, and skip hits on continuation
+       bytes. */
+    while (s < text_range) {
+      if (map[*s] != 0 && (*s & 0xc0) != 0x80) return (UChar* )s;
+      s++;
+    }
+    return (UChar* )NULL;
+  }
+
